":"VRCE-6-75-22","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":75,"verse":22,"english":"This very day shall the exceedingly wicked Råma behold you, who are the friend of Kș atriyas and devoted to your brother, and who ėver bear yourself ignobly, slain by me; today see you, O Sumitra's son, slain by me, with your armour fallen off your person, your bow broken, and your head severed.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.95,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-88-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6075022","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"61b650e28b5f72e668e348bd8b3e3277933964b54f3eebf81a97e340dad548b6","compact":"b58786b526f168402216c3adca8fd18297b2da6fe7477ec1688ca457e1719b79","nasal":"40336a60c5b0e96a3de4b29b65dddb335663e74b5104c85b23c339440ea106b3"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-75-23","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":75,"verse":23,"english":"As Ravana's son was speaking thus harshly, Lakşmaņa conversant with the import of words, replied in a reasonable speech, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.515,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-88-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6075023","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e7144956d9574513abb07636f01c9da4b34e10c8eb7ad4dd9f243f719ff04ae7","compact":"5f1c57370676115c21976f04acef60203dff9671916e05cafc48567581b34bb2","nasal":"5f1c57370676115c21976f04acef60203dff9671916e05cafc48567581b34bb2"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-75-24","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":75,"verse":24,"english":"Why, O Raksasa, without doing (what you say), did you simply say it? Do the deed, so that I may have regard for your utterances.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.806,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-88-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6075024","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Du